TaffyGo's product-manifest overlay. The pinned Chromium base supplies the
browser application and its ordinary platform declarations; these blocks add
only TaffyGo-owned policy and components.

The base emits android:allowBackup="false" whenever no backup_key is supplied.
//taffy/app/android/product_targets.gni deliberately supplies none. The two
rules below are still required because Android 12+ and some OEMs treat direct
device transfer separately from that attribute.
#}

{% block extra_uses_permissions %}
{{ super() }}
<uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
<!--
  The queries below name the three things this browser has to be able to see:
  an app that handles a web link, the viewer the PDF task hand-off opens
  (TaffyTaskPdfHandoff.kt, the only PackageManager query this product's own
  code makes), and the Play Store. The Play Store entry outlived both reasons
  decision 0153 gave for it — the plan surfaces are deleted and Play Billing
  is gone — and it stays for a reason that record does not state.

  Two readings of why it stays are wrong, and both are easy to reach. It is
  not clause 4's Play Store listing for an `intent://` naming an uninstalled
  package: that is upstream's ExternalNavigationHandler, and it never runs
  here, because TaffyExternalNavigationDelegate.shouldDisableAllExternalIntents
  returns true and TaffyExternalNavigationHandler.shouldOverrideUrlLoading
  never calls its superclass. It is not the `market://` hand-off either
  (TaffyExternalAppHandoff, TaffyExternalAppHandoffTest): that goes out through
  Intent.createChooser and startActivity, which package visibility does not
  filter.

  What does need it is upstream code this product compiles and never wrote:
  PasswordManagerUtilBridge.isGooglePlayServicesUpdatable reads
  PackageUtils.getPackageInfo("com.android.vending"), which visibility
  filtering does answer with null. Upstream needs no entry of its own because
  it declares QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES; this product declines that permission, so
  every package upstream expects to see has to be named here or stop existing
  as far as that code can tell. Searching taffy-core/ alone says this entry
  has no consumer, and that census is narrower than the artifact.
-->
<queries>
    <intent>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <data android:scheme="content" android:mimeType="application/pdf" />
    </intent>
    <intent>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <data android:scheme="https" />
    </intent>
    <intent>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <data android:scheme="http" />
    </intent>
    <package android:name="com.android.vending" />
</queries>
{% endblock %}

{#
TaffyGo does not enumerate what a person has installed (decision 0153).
QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES is upstream's, declared so Chrome can decide whether an app
installed on the device should handle a web navigation; on API 30 and above it
switches package-visibility filtering off for the whole application, which is
an inventory of the device this product has no use for. Upstream patch 0051
wraps that declaration in the block overridden here, because the manifest
merger's tools:node="remove" reaches only elements arriving from another
manifest and is ignored for one this document declares. Overriding it with
nothing narrows external hand-off rather than ending it: a navigation whose
scheme no query above names stays in the browser, follows the page's
browser_fallback_url, or — for an intent:// URL naming a package — offers that
package's Play Store listing.
#}
{% block query_all_packages_permission %}{% endblock %}

{#
The camera, microphone and media-projection foreground-service types are
upstream's, for MediaCaptureNotificationService. The only path that starts it
in the foreground with them is background media capture, which upstream turns
on for desktop Android builds alone and the feature posture turns off by name
(taffy-core/browser/feature_posture.cc). On a phone a site's call stops when
the browser leaves the screen, so the three were declared and never used, and
Play asks for a declaration and a video for each type a manifest carries.
Upstream patch 0052 puts them in the two blocks overridden here with nothing,
for the reason patch 0051 records (decision 0252, point 6).
#}
{% block media_capture_foreground_service_permissions %}{% endblock %}
{% block media_capture_foreground_service_type %}{% endblock %}

{% block extra_application_attributes %}
{{ super() }}
android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/taffy_data_extraction_rules"
android:fullBackupContent="@xml/taffy_full_backup_content"
{% endblock %}

{% block extra_application_definitions %}
{{ super() }}
<service
    android:name="com.taffygo.browser.ui.app.TaskContinuationService"
    android:exported="false"
    android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
    <property
        android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
        android:value="user_initiated_browser_research_with_visible_pause_stop_resume" />
</service>
<receiver
    android:name="org.chromium.taffy.shell.TaffyDownloadActionReceiver"
    android:exported="false" />
<!--
  Nothing about Cast is removed here, and that is a decision rather than an
  omission. A previous revision of this template removed
  MediaIntentReceiver and the policy_cast_dynamite query, on the rationale that
  they rode in on the datatransport carrier that the Play Billing library
  vendored here depended on, and that TaffyGo has no Cast surface. That
  library has since been removed from the product and both components are
  still in the merged manifest, which settles the carrier half by measurement
  rather than by argument. Neither half survives contact with a built artifact: an APK built
  before that library was vendored already carried both, from chrome_java's own
  Cast dependency, and the same merged manifest registers CastOptionsProvider
  and ChromeMediaNotificationControllerServices$CastListenerService, with
  nothing in taffy-core/ or chromium/ disabling media_router. Upstream removes
  ReconnectionService by name ("Disabled via CastOptionsProvider") and keeps
  MediaIntentReceiver on purpose, recording it in its own golden manifest
  expectations. Deleting shipping components from the product on a false
  rationale is worse than carrying them, so they stay. What TaffyGo's
  media-router posture should be is an open question for its owner and wants
  its own evidence and its own decision record; it is not a billing change's to
  settle.
-->
{% endblock %}
